main='''<main id="main"><div class="atlas-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Overview</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/evidence/">Evidence</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Quadrature budget</span></div><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">21 October 2026 · Continuous-band certificate 0.1</div><h1>A narrow source class.<br>A continuous-band bound.</h1><p class="lede">The unchanged measured-window kernel satisfies a proposed ±50 Hz source and uncertainty budget using exact rational interval enclosures and an analytic remainder.</p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>Exact conditional certificate · Development — no registry entry · Physical promotion 0</strong><p>Source and instrument premises are requirements, not measured capabilities. Independent review is pending. No empirical rerun, native-action estimate or promotion; EXIT-03 HOLD and prior 2/6 and 0/891 outcomes remain intact.</p></aside><div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/sources/quadrature-budget-v0.1/THEOREM.md">Read theorem and proof ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/quadrature-budget-v0.1/CONTRACT.json">Frozen source and instrument requirements</a></div><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>The source premise is explicit.</h2><p>The complex envelope has spectral support within ±50 Hz relative to the fixed carrier and complex spectral-measure total variation at most 1 V. True amplitude must be at least 1/2 V wherever phase is reported. Combined input error must be at most 1/2000 V; constant baseline magnitude is at most 3 V.</p><p>These are proposed design restrictions. They are stronger than observing a spectral peak or a finite-record RMS bandwidth. The earlier faster-envelope and pulse cases are not silently readmitted.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Conditional bound</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Total complex error</td><td>&lt;0.005788 V</td><td>1/100 V</td></tr><tr><td>Phase error</td><td>&lt;0.011693 rad</td><td>1/50 rad</td></tr><tr><td>Availability delay</td><td>524.288 microseconds</td><td>600 microseconds</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>Every frequency in the declared band is covered.</h2><p>The proof bounds main-response distortion and conjugate-image leakage with center moments, first derivatives and a global second-derivative remainder. Saved binary64 kernel coefficients are interpreted as exact dyadics; no coefficient refit occurs.</p><p>Integer-directed 160-bit intervals enclose trigonometric moments using Machin’s pi formula and explicit Taylor remainders. Exact fractions carry the comparisons; displayed decimals are explanatory. The separate rounding allowance applies to a declared serial binary64 dot product, not arbitrary libraries or all platforms.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/quadrature-budget-v0.1/CERTIFICATE.json">Exact certificate fractions →</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/quadrature-budget-v0.1/certify.py">Standard-Python certificate replay ↓</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>A concrete failure stays visible.</h2><p>A 600 Hz unit tone has a certified complex-error lower bound above 0.2305 for this kernel, so it fails 1% recovery. This is not a no-go against other kernels. A larger input-error allowance fails the sufficient budget, while a 500 microsecond delay cap rejects the unchanged window.</p><p>Next derive a leakage-tolerant source contract and allocate the remaining margin to spectral tails and instrument uncertainty. Source bandwidth, noise, gain/skew, full energy, absolute native support and action normalization remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/quadrature-budget-v0.1/REPORT.md">Requirements and limits →</a><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/local-quadrature/">Preserved local estimator →</a></div></section></main>'''
